we have a gap for 2021.

00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:09.177
Also quick reminder,

00:01:09.177 --> 00:01:11.874
you can get a version of this live stream,

00:01:11.874 --> 00:01:13.587
"Homeroom with Sal, the podcast,"

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:15.857
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:19.267
So with that, I'm excited
to introduce Dan Roth.

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.147
Dan, good to see you.

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:23.280
- Great to see you,
thanks for having me on.

00:01:24.257 --> 00:01:27.037
- So maybe the first thing,
just to get everyone acquainted,

00:01:27.037 --> 00:01:29.717
I think most people know about LinkedIn.

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:32.437
And I think most folks, you
know, at the high level,

00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:35.237
it's kind of the social
network for business.

00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:38.127
They view it as a place
where you can look for jobs,

00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:40.117
find jobs, find interesting things

00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:42.717
that are going on in the business world.

00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:46.757
Why does LinkedIn have a Editor in Chief?

00:01:46.757 --> 00:01:48.777
What is it you do?
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:01:48.777 --> 00:01:51.867
It's a great question, and I
ask myself that all the time.

00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:55.467
So I have been at LinkedIn
for almost 10 years now,

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:56.527
nine and a half years.

00:01:56.527 --> 00:02:00.667
I joined LinkedIn when LinkedIn was moving

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:04.917
from being a place to connect
professionals with each other,

00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:07.357
to being a place that
serves as a way for people

00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:09.757
to connect to economic opportunity.

00:02:09.757 --> 00:02:11.847
And at the time before joining LinkedIn,

00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:14.697
I was a long time writer and editor

00:02:14.697 --> 00:02:17.357
at "Fortune," "Wired," "Forbes."

00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:20.157
I'd help start a magazine
called Conde Nast Portfolio,

00:02:20.157 --> 00:02:22.857
which was a business magazine
started by Conde Nast,

00:02:22.857 --> 00:02:25.747
and had always been a business journalist

00:02:25.747 --> 00:02:29.817
just trying to explain to the
world and to professionals

00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:31.190
how business worked.

00:02:32.997 --> 00:02:36.127
At the time, LinkedIn Jeff Weiner,

00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:37.540
who was the CEO of LinkedIn,

00:02:38.777 --> 00:02:41.130
was thinking about the
next evolution of LinkedIn.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.267
And his thinking was that if we are going

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.887
to serve the mission of LinkedIn,

00:02:45.887 --> 00:02:47.207
which is not just to connect people

00:02:47.207 --> 00:02:48.887
but to help make them
better at what they do

00:02:48.887 --> 00:02:50.267
or what they want to do,

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:52.117
we do that by helping them stay informed

00:02:52.117 --> 00:02:52.967
and helping make sure that

00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:54.677
they are building their own voice.

00:02:54.677 --> 00:02:58.237
And the way that you do
that is through getting them

00:02:58.237 --> 00:02:59.747
the right news at the right time,

00:02:59.747 --> 00:03:01.007
and then getting them to talk about it.

00:03:01.007 --> 00:03:03.797
And so to do that well,
you need an editor.

00:03:03.797 --> 00:03:06.097
So he hired me and I built up a team.

00:03:06.097 --> 00:03:09.117
We have about 75 editors around the world

00:03:09.117 --> 00:03:12.717
who curate content, cultivate new voices,

00:03:12.717 --> 00:03:15.217
or trying to find people
who are uniquely qualified

00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:18.267
to talk about certain things
and bring them on LinkedIn.

00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:21.057
And then we curate the
best of what we see.

00:03:21.057 --> 00:03:22.850
So curate, cultivate, create.

00:03:23.717 --> 00:03:25.797
These are mostly business
journalists like myself,

00:03:25.797 --> 00:03:27.257
and we are just trying to make sure that

00:03:27.257 --> 00:03:29.607
we are serving up high
quality information to people

00:03:29.607 --> 00:03:31.547
so that when you come to LinkedIn,

00:03:31.547 --> 00:03:33.130
your time is spent,

00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:36.867
that is a valuable use of your time.

00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:39.017
That if you are no matter,
what you do for a living,

00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:40.947
when you come to LinkedIn,
you should find some news

00:03:40.947 --> 00:03:42.837
that's interesting to you that helps you

00:03:42.837 --> 00:03:44.527
get smarter at what you're doing

00:03:44.527 --> 00:03:45.947
or helps you make a connection

00:03:45.947 --> 00:03:48.357
or connect dots from around the world.

00:03:48.357 --> 00:03:51.317
And to do that well, you
need editors and algorithms.

00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:54.317
So we work really closely
with our relevance engineers,

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.967
we make sure that we are giving people

00:03:56.967 --> 00:03:57.967
what they want in their world,

00:03:57.967 --> 00:03:59.077
but also helping them

00:03:59.077 --> 00:04:00.647
helping pop filter bubbles,

00:04:00.647 --> 00:04:01.647
and make sure they are seeing

00:04:01.647 --> 00:04:03.257
what is going on in the world around them.

00:04:03.257 --> 00:04:04.107
That's what I do.

00:04:05.287 --> 00:04:07.487
- It's fascinating because
it's a very different take

00:04:07.487 --> 00:04:09.837
than some of the other
social media platforms,

00:04:09.837 --> 00:04:11.497
obviously that aren't
quite as business focused.

00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:14.050
But if you take a Twitter,
if you take a Facebook,

00:04:15.197 --> 00:04:18.717
you know, the news is in
theory all user-generated,

00:04:18.717 --> 00:04:22.187
and they have increasingly
fallen under scrutiny of,

00:04:22.187 --> 00:04:24.297
hey, what if someone
posts something that is,

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.907
you know, inciting violence
or it's just inaccurate.

00:04:26.907 --> 00:04:28.347
And so they they're having
to do a little bit more

00:04:28.347 --> 00:04:29.787
policing function but they're definitely

00:04:29.787 --> 00:04:31.990
not creating their own content.

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:35.337
Why do you think LinkedIn
has gone down this path,

00:04:35.337 --> 00:04:36.637
and what do you think has been

00:04:36.637 --> 00:04:38.587
the pros and cons of doing that?

00:04:38.587 --> 00:04:39.420
- Sure.

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:46.397
It is important to us that
when people come to LinkedIn,

00:04:46.397 --> 00:04:48.537
they know that this is a place

00:04:48.537 --> 00:04:49.997
where they can trust what
they see in LinkedIn.

00:04:49.997 --> 00:04:53.147
If you look, the Business
Insider does a survey every year

00:04:53.147 --> 00:04:56.337
of social, of trust in social media,

00:04:56.337 --> 00:04:58.447
LinkedIn for, I don't
know, four years running,

00:04:58.447 --> 00:04:59.427
three, four years running,

00:04:59.427 --> 00:05:03.777
is the number one most
trusted social media site.

00:05:03.777 --> 00:05:06.517
The way that we do that and
the way that we maintain it

00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:08.357
is by one, making sure
we have real identities.

00:05:08.357 --> 00:05:09.267
When you come to LinkedIn,

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:12.067
you are coming with your
professional identity,

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:13.382
you are saying who you work for,

00:05:13.382 --> 00:05:15.757
who you have worked for in the past.

00:05:15.757 --> 00:05:17.467
And then we are connecting you

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:19.287
with your colleagues who can vouch for you

00:05:19.287 --> 00:05:21.447
or talk about what you're good at also.

00:05:21.447 --> 00:05:22.797
When you come to your feed,

00:05:22.797 --> 00:05:24.087
you should have a feeling that,

00:05:24.087 --> 00:05:25.507
just like you're seeing real people,

00:05:25.507 --> 00:05:28.227
that you are seeing real news
and information on there.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:31.767
So we have always believed
that it is important

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:33.207
to have an editorial voice

00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:36.347
and an editorial perspective on LinkedIn.

00:05:36.347 --> 00:05:38.450
So for instance, with the latest election,

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.553
if you open your app right now

00:05:40.553 --> 00:05:42.507
and you go to your notifications tab,

00:05:42.507 --> 00:05:45.144
you'll see something at the top that says,

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:48.277
"The 2020 election," "The
latest on the 2020 election."

00:05:48.277 --> 00:05:49.227
If you click there,

00:05:50.263 --> 00:05:51.977
if you're in the US I should say,

00:05:51.977 --> 00:05:54.457
if you click there,
you'll end up on a page

00:05:54.457 --> 00:05:57.507
that is created and curated by editors

00:05:57.507 --> 00:05:58.637
that says here is exactly

00:05:58.637 --> 00:05:59.777
what is going on with the election.

00:05:59.777 --> 00:06:02.667
This is trusted information
from trusted sources.

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:07.127
And we'd ran this all the way
through the election process.

00:06:07.127 --> 00:06:08.807
And it's still up there today.

00:06:08.807 --> 00:06:11.637
It is important that if you
are, no matter what you do,

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:12.977
if you come to LinkedIn,

00:06:12.977 --> 00:06:15.047
that you have that one place
to go to get that information

00:06:15.047 --> 00:06:17.057
and say, here is what's
going on in the world.

00:06:17.057 --> 00:06:19.377
I can trust what I'm seeing here.

00:06:19.377 --> 00:06:20.967
You asked about UGC,

00:06:20.967 --> 00:06:25.077
user-generated content is the
heart and soul of LinkedIn.

00:06:25.077 --> 00:06:27.997
What we create as editors
is a drop in the bucket

00:06:27.997 --> 00:06:30.427
versus what our members
create every single day

00:06:30.427 --> 00:06:33.987
through their articles
and their posts, videos,

00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:36.347
images that they're
putting up on LinkedIn.

00:06:36.347 --> 00:06:38.637
They are talking to their world about

00:06:38.637 --> 00:06:41.477
what's going on around them
and what they're seeing.

00:06:41.477 --> 00:06:43.067
And we as editors are trying to get

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:45.367
more and more of that content from them.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:47.497
So we'll reach out every day to people

00:06:49.788 --> 00:06:52.517
who might be in the news,
whether they know it or not.

00:06:52.517 --> 00:06:54.287
So let's say for instance that

00:06:55.127 --> 00:06:57.277
there's a big merger going on.

00:06:57.277 --> 00:06:59.127
We could reach out to people
who are in that industry

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:00.797
saying, can you explain what,

00:07:00.797 --> 00:07:03.137
you know, Spotify is buying Megaphone,

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:04.537
can you talk about what this means

00:07:04.537 --> 00:07:06.857
for the future of podcasting?

00:07:06.857 --> 00:07:08.607
And people who are in
the podcasting business

00:07:08.607 --> 00:07:10.267
would get this alert
and they might say like,

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:12.517
oh yeah, I have something
that I am qualified

00:07:12.517 --> 00:07:14.377
to talk about, that I want to share.

00:07:14.377 --> 00:07:15.767
There's something that's
in the back of my head

00:07:15.767 --> 00:07:17.447
that I've talked to my colleagues about,

00:07:17.447 --> 00:07:19.487
but if I can share it with
the world, maybe something,

00:07:19.487 --> 00:07:21.597
maybe an even better conversation happens.

00:07:21.597 --> 00:07:24.257
And so we get them to
share that on LinkedIn.

00:07:24.257 --> 00:07:26.667
And then, I think the last point is that

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:28.967
you asked about like
how this is different.

00:07:28.967 --> 00:07:29.907
The big difference is that

00:07:29.907 --> 00:07:33.067
we're a professional social media site.

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:35.477
What you write here is seen by your boss

00:07:35.477 --> 00:07:38.127
and your colleagues and
your future colleagues

00:07:38.127 --> 00:07:40.807
and people who you might
want to work for you one day

00:07:40.807 --> 00:07:42.457
when you start your own business.

00:07:43.317 --> 00:07:47.727
All of that increases the, I think-

00:07:47.727 --> 00:07:51.147
Oh for a lot of us, sharing
on social is always worrisome.

00:07:51.147 --> 00:07:52.287
But when you come to LinkedIn,

00:07:52.287 --> 00:07:54.127
you know, there's that extra concern.

00:07:54.127 --> 00:07:56.747
What is my boss gonna say when
they see what I've written?

00:07:56.747 --> 00:07:58.617
What are my employees gonna say?

00:07:58.617 --> 00:08:02.957
And so we as editors can
help guide people to say

00:08:02.957 --> 00:08:05.367
this is a topic that is
worth weighing in on.

00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:07.457
People are hungry to hear from you

00:08:07.457 --> 00:08:09.277
on this thing that's going on in the news.

00:08:09.277 --> 00:08:11.857
And this is a safe thing to talk about.

00:08:11.857 --> 00:08:13.927
So I think unlike some other social media

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:16.197
where you might fire it up and talk about

00:08:16.197 --> 00:08:18.087
whatever you feel like talking about,

00:08:18.087 --> 00:08:22.297
there's an extra level of
concern and seriousness

00:08:22.297 --> 00:08:23.336
that takes place on LinkedIn

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:25.300
because of the professional profile.

00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:29.137
- I'm curious, and I
definitely want to talk about

00:08:29.137 --> 00:08:30.997
the future of work which
I know is another area

00:08:30.997 --> 00:08:32.047
that you have a lot of expertise,

00:08:32.047 --> 00:08:34.067
but, you know, this whole
notion of social media

00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:35.857
and misinformation, it's such a hot topic.

00:08:35.857 --> 00:08:37.407
And it does seem like LinkedIn

00:08:37.407 --> 00:08:39.547
has threaded the needle quite well

00:08:39.547 --> 00:08:41.947
of having a lot of user-generated content,

00:08:41.947 --> 00:08:44.217
also having some
editorially curated content,

00:08:44.217 --> 00:08:45.437
and also just by the nature of it

00:08:45.437 --> 00:08:47.347
being real identities, et
cetera, everything you mentioned.

00:08:47.347 --> 00:08:49.586
It seems like you're doing a good job.

00:08:49.586 --> 00:08:50.677
What do you think is the future

00:08:50.677 --> 00:08:52.517
of some of the other
social media platforms?

00:08:52.517 --> 00:08:54.247
I mean, I actually, you know,

00:08:54.247 --> 00:08:55.567
I've thought a lot about this issue.

00:08:55.567 --> 00:08:58.627
I don't have a simple
solution for, you know,

00:08:58.627 --> 00:09:01.497
how do you balance people being
able to put ideas out there

00:09:01.497 --> 00:09:05.317
while some ideas are from Russian bots,

00:09:05.317 --> 00:09:07.757
or just people who are trying
to put out misinformation?

00:09:07.757 --> 00:09:09.557
Where do you think this is gonna go?

00:09:13.127 --> 00:09:14.427
- You know, I wish I knew.

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:17.687
I can tell you that my own beliefs

00:09:17.687 --> 00:09:19.417
about what's important
for us to figure out

00:09:19.417 --> 00:09:21.957
in the social media landscape
is how do we make sure

00:09:23.013 --> 00:09:25.127
that there are real
identities behind everything.

00:09:25.127 --> 00:09:26.737
That I think that there is-

00:09:26.737 --> 00:09:31.737
One of the key drivers
to LinkedIn's success

00:09:31.847 --> 00:09:33.207
has been this idea that

00:09:33.207 --> 00:09:37.190
you have to stand for what you are saying.

00:09:38.327 --> 00:09:41.547
There are some people that
don't mind saying crazy things

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:43.007
in knowing that it's
under a real identity,

00:09:43.007 --> 00:09:44.087
that is always gonna happen,

00:09:44.087 --> 00:09:45.737
but it definitely raises the bar.

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:50.947
It should make you think twice

00:09:50.947 --> 00:09:52.657
about what it is that you're saying.

00:09:52.657 --> 00:09:54.187
So I think that's really important.

00:09:54.187 --> 00:09:58.447
And I think that what the other
social media sites are doing

00:09:58.447 --> 00:10:00.547
in terms of labeling
content is really important.

00:10:00.547 --> 00:10:03.427
I think the role of fact
checkers is important.

00:10:03.427 --> 00:10:04.517
I hope that social media sites

00:10:04.517 --> 00:10:07.130
keep investing in those areas.

00:10:08.673 --> 00:10:10.094
And then I guess the
last thing I would say

00:10:10.094 --> 00:10:11.717
is that I think that-

00:10:11.717 --> 00:10:13.947
I watch how my kids use social,

00:10:13.947 --> 00:10:15.257
and it's very different

00:10:17.505 --> 00:10:19.607
than the way I think older
generations use social.

00:10:19.607 --> 00:10:22.507
They go into it with a
certain understanding

00:10:23.883 --> 00:10:25.544
of what it's for and what it's not for,

00:10:25.544 --> 00:10:27.207
and when to take it seriously

00:10:27.207 --> 00:10:28.317
and when not to take it seriously.

00:10:28.317 --> 00:10:30.470
And also I think when to hop off.

00:10:31.857 --> 00:10:34.187
And I wonder if this generation

00:10:34.187 --> 00:10:35.727
that has grown up with social

00:10:35.727 --> 00:10:37.990
as being the primary way that they are,

00:10:38.837 --> 00:10:41.427
not just getting information
but building their voice,

00:10:41.427 --> 00:10:46.427
has a better handle on
what they're seeing there.

00:10:46.477 --> 00:10:48.287
That, because they are also the ones

00:10:48.287 --> 00:10:50.160
putting up jokey content,

00:10:51.167 --> 00:10:53.757
that they can recognize
when someone is trolling.

00:10:53.757 --> 00:10:56.107
Because maybe they're
trolling themselves also.

00:10:57.448 --> 00:11:01.490
And so I do think there will be a change

00:11:03.587 --> 00:11:05.790
in how damaging social can be.

00:11:07.007 --> 00:11:11.077
Or the downsides, when people
have much more information

00:11:11.077 --> 00:11:15.977
or they're intrinsically
get what the weaknesses are

00:11:15.977 --> 00:11:17.947
with what they might be
seeing on certain sites

00:11:17.947 --> 00:11:19.527
where there is no anonymity,

00:11:19.527 --> 00:11:20.837
where anyone can post anything

00:11:20.837 --> 00:11:21.957
and where people are saying stuff

00:11:21.957 --> 00:11:23.457
just to get a rise out of you.

00:11:24.387 --> 00:11:27.057
- Yeah, yeah, and I hope you're right.

00:11:27.057 --> 00:11:29.257
I mean, we've had guests
on before talking about,

00:11:29.257 --> 00:11:31.627
that this generation that's
growing up on social media,

00:11:31.627 --> 00:11:33.037
all of the negatives that it might have,

00:11:33.037 --> 00:11:35.837
but they might also be
building some skillsets.

00:11:35.837 --> 00:11:37.287
So, you know, pivoting a little bit,

00:11:37.287 --> 00:11:40.347
we're obviously in the
midst of a COVID crisis,

00:11:40.347 --> 00:11:41.627
it looks like you're working from home,

00:11:41.627 --> 00:11:42.727
I'm working from home.

00:11:44.377 --> 00:11:47.527
What's your view, you know,
what are you seeing in LinkedIn?

00:11:47.527 --> 00:11:48.377
What are you doing in reporting?

00:11:48.377 --> 00:11:52.500
What's your view that you're
having of the future of work,

00:11:53.533 --> 00:11:55.883
and COVID especially,
how it might impact that?

00:11:57.797 --> 00:12:02.417
- I think that it's a big question.

00:12:02.417 --> 00:12:05.720
And I think there are a lot of
different ways to look at it.

00:12:07.067 --> 00:12:08.357
There are certain industries

00:12:08.357 --> 00:12:10.627
that we have seen through our data

00:12:10.627 --> 00:12:13.120
that are just getting absolutely,

00:12:14.718 --> 00:12:16.517
that are in such serious pain,

00:12:16.517 --> 00:12:19.367
that I think it's hard to
think about how they come back.

00:12:20.287 --> 00:12:23.847
And there are others that
are doing extremely well.

00:12:23.847 --> 00:12:25.207
- What are the ones that you think

00:12:25.207 --> 00:12:26.897
might have trouble coming back?

00:12:26.897 --> 00:12:29.667
- Well, I think leisure and hospitality,

00:12:29.667 --> 00:12:31.237
it's gonna be a long time
before they come back.

00:12:31.237 --> 00:12:32.927
And there could be a point where

00:12:32.927 --> 00:12:34.407
coming back is no longer an option

00:12:34.407 --> 00:12:37.867
because you have lost years of knowledge.

00:12:37.867 --> 00:12:39.527
You've had to cut to the bone.

00:12:39.527 --> 00:12:41.420
You have lost people's-

00:12:44.198 --> 00:12:45.137
How people spend their money

00:12:45.137 --> 00:12:47.237
and what they do in their
leisure time has changed.

00:12:47.237 --> 00:12:48.517
So I think it's gonna be years

00:12:48.517 --> 00:12:52.877
before business travel comes back.

00:12:52.877 --> 00:12:54.307
And because I think we're all learning

00:12:54.307 --> 00:12:56.947
how to do these kinds of calls from home.

00:12:56.947 --> 00:13:00.237
There was a, I think it
was the CEO of Infosys,

00:13:00.237 --> 00:13:05.237
massive Indian outsourcing IT firm,

00:13:06.737 --> 00:13:10.887
that said look, I used to
get on a plane and fly,

00:13:10.887 --> 00:13:15.167
and I would fly to the
US for a $5 million deal.

00:13:15.167 --> 00:13:18.030
Now we're closing $2
billion deals over VC,

00:13:19.039 --> 00:13:20.607
over these kind of calls.

00:13:20.607 --> 00:13:22.347
One, you realize you have to do that,

00:13:22.347 --> 00:13:23.437
you don't have to go pack a bag,

00:13:23.437 --> 00:13:24.437
you don't have to get trapped

00:13:24.437 --> 00:13:25.987
in an airplane for eight hours,

00:13:25.987 --> 00:13:27.117
you don't have to deal with jet lag,

00:13:27.117 --> 00:13:29.017
going back to that's really difficult.

00:13:30.712 --> 00:13:32.370
I do an interview series also on LinkedIn,

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:33.547
I was talking to David Chang,

00:13:33.547 --> 00:13:35.947
the founder of Momofuku yesterday,

00:13:35.947 --> 00:13:38.257
and he's a big giant restaurateur,

00:13:38.257 --> 00:13:40.930
and he says, look,
without business travel,

00:13:41.827 --> 00:13:43.227
restaurants don't come back.

00:13:43.227 --> 00:13:44.757
We've relied on that lunch traffic

00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:45.747
and that breakfast traffic

00:13:45.747 --> 00:13:48.197
to be able to pay for so much else

00:13:48.197 --> 00:13:50.447
of what goes on in the restaurant,
in the restaurant world.

00:13:50.447 --> 00:13:52.077
It's not just leisure dinners.

00:13:52.077 --> 00:13:54.067
And so you start seeing
these downstream impacts

00:13:54.067 --> 00:13:56.767
where a lot of leisure
and travel don't come back

00:13:56.767 --> 00:13:58.717
if business travel doesn't come back.

00:13:58.717 --> 00:14:00.347
Cruises, when do cruises come back?

00:14:00.347 --> 00:14:02.287
I think that's gonna be a long time.

00:14:02.287 --> 00:14:06.357
So anything that involves us
getting together in person,

00:14:06.357 --> 00:14:08.697
I think, will take a while to come back

00:14:08.697 --> 00:14:10.157
and will come back in a different form.

00:14:10.157 --> 00:14:11.517
What we've seen on LinkedIn is the people

00:14:11.517 --> 00:14:15.747
who are in those industries
are very actively

00:14:15.747 --> 00:14:17.477
trying to get out of those industries.

00:14:17.477 --> 00:14:21.207
They've realized that there
is a need to find a path

00:14:21.207 --> 00:14:24.670
that takes you from this
area into something else.

00:14:26.564 --> 00:14:28.567
And I think that's accelerating-

00:14:28.567 --> 00:14:31.257
You know, I don't know if
you've had Professor Galloway,

00:14:31.257 --> 00:14:33.167
Scott Galloway, on your show before,

00:14:33.167 --> 00:14:36.507
but he has this theory
that what COVID is doing

00:14:36.507 --> 00:14:39.627
is just accelerating trends
that were already in the works.

00:14:39.627 --> 00:14:43.310
So virtual big tech getting bigger,

00:14:46.039 --> 00:14:49.507
and I would add to that this
kind of skills-based workforce,

00:14:49.507 --> 00:14:54.507
this idea that what you do is
really a collection of skills

00:14:56.047 --> 00:15:00.437
versus you being an expert
in one particular job.

00:15:00.437 --> 00:15:01.787
So, as an example of that, like,

00:15:01.787 --> 00:15:04.877
you could be a great travel-

00:15:04.877 --> 00:15:08.647
You might be a great, I
don't know, person in travel.

00:15:08.647 --> 00:15:10.090
You might be a good travel,

00:15:11.667 --> 00:15:13.947
anything in travel, and you
have to just think about

00:15:13.947 --> 00:15:17.987
what the atomized set of skills
are that make up your job,

00:15:17.987 --> 00:15:20.557
and then where else you
could apply those skills.

00:15:20.557 --> 00:15:22.817
I think my favorite example of this is,

00:15:22.817 --> 00:15:25.787
I was talking to someone
at a insurance company

00:15:25.787 --> 00:15:30.787
who said that when they look
for insurance adjusters,

00:15:30.787 --> 00:15:35.787
people that can work with
people around their accidents,

00:15:37.187 --> 00:15:39.387
and try to understand what
happened and who's at fault

00:15:39.387 --> 00:15:42.617
and whatever else goes into
being an insurance adjuster,

00:15:42.617 --> 00:15:46.517
this executive said, we hire bartenders.

00:15:46.517 --> 00:15:48.407
Because bartenders have this ability

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.737
to get people to tell their stories.

00:15:51.737 --> 00:15:52.987
And I think if you're a bartender,

00:15:52.987 --> 00:15:57.047
you don't think of yourself
as being a story aggregator.

00:15:57.047 --> 00:15:57.937
But that's a skill.

00:15:57.937 --> 00:15:59.747
It's a skill to get people to talk.

00:15:59.747 --> 00:16:01.447
And once you start thinking of your work

00:16:01.447 --> 00:16:03.887
in terms of what are the skills
that go into what do I do,

00:16:03.887 --> 00:16:07.537
versus I identify myself as a bartender,

00:16:07.537 --> 00:16:09.287
the world starts opening up to you.

00:16:09.287 --> 00:16:11.607
And I think that is accelerated by COVID,

00:16:11.607 --> 00:16:14.947
is people realizing that
they've built their careers

00:16:14.947 --> 00:16:17.157
on potentially shaky ground,

00:16:17.157 --> 00:16:18.867
and that if they are
going to continue to work

00:16:18.867 --> 00:16:20.777
it is gonna come from being able to hop

00:16:20.777 --> 00:16:23.067
from industry to industry and job to job.

00:16:23.067 --> 00:16:25.650
And it's your skills that
will enable you to do that.

00:16:26.557 --> 00:16:28.807
- And what do you think about
the in-person workplace?

00:16:28.807 --> 00:16:30.177
What do you think about, you know,

00:16:30.177 --> 00:16:31.747
out here in Silicon
Valley some people say,

00:16:31.747 --> 00:16:35.537
oh commercial real estate is gonna die.

00:16:35.537 --> 00:16:36.927
Other people say, no it
might be bigger than ever

00:16:36.927 --> 00:16:39.268
because people need more space.

00:16:39.268 --> 00:16:41.187
Where do you fall on that debate?

00:16:41.187 --> 00:16:42.527
- All right, so this is
just a personal take,

00:16:42.527 --> 00:16:46.207
but I think that it is a mistake

00:16:46.207 --> 00:16:48.727
to write off personal interactions.

00:16:48.727 --> 00:16:50.547
I think the workplace
might change in how we,

00:16:50.547 --> 00:16:53.297
what we do in work, it will
become a lot more flexible.

00:16:54.697 --> 00:16:59.467
But I think that this
need for human interaction

00:16:59.467 --> 00:17:03.037
and the kind of ideas that
come through serendipity,

00:17:03.037 --> 00:17:05.187
through bumping into someone in a hallway,

00:17:05.187 --> 00:17:08.097
through the meeting that
takes place after the meeting.

00:17:08.097 --> 00:17:10.687
You know, some of the best
projects we've ever done

00:17:10.687 --> 00:17:11.877
that I've been involved with,

00:17:11.877 --> 00:17:13.547
have come because the meeting is over

00:17:13.547 --> 00:17:15.647
and a few of us stay
in the conference room

00:17:15.647 --> 00:17:17.467
and all of a sudden someone
gets up to a whiteboard,

00:17:17.467 --> 00:17:19.557
and says, you know actually,
what if we do it this way?

00:17:19.557 --> 00:17:20.637
And you're like oh yeah,

00:17:20.637 --> 00:17:22.177
that's exactly the way we should do it.

00:17:22.177 --> 00:17:23.160
And off you go.

00:17:24.237 --> 00:17:27.187
When you are doing these
kinds of teams or Zoom calls,

00:17:27.187 --> 00:17:28.767
one half hour or half hour, half hour,

00:17:28.767 --> 00:17:30.147
which is how a lot of us are working,

00:17:30.147 --> 00:17:32.127
it's how I'm working right now,

00:17:32.127 --> 00:17:34.397
you lose that spontaneity.

00:17:34.397 --> 00:17:37.797
And I think there is a
real benefit for that,

00:17:37.797 --> 00:17:39.517
and I think that we're really missing it.

00:17:39.517 --> 00:17:43.517
So this morning, I got a chance
to go on a walking meeting

00:17:43.517 --> 00:17:45.727
with someone who I haven't met before.

00:17:45.727 --> 00:17:47.864
And we were planning on getting together,

00:17:47.864 --> 00:17:50.327
it was the first time
I've done it since March.

00:17:50.327 --> 00:17:51.667
We met in Prospect Park,

00:17:51.667 --> 00:17:53.987
I'm based in New York, we met
in Prospect Park in Brooklyn,

00:17:53.987 --> 00:17:56.147
and we walked side-by-side, our masks on.

00:17:56.147 --> 00:17:57.407
And it was for 45 minutes.

00:17:57.407 --> 00:18:01.097
We just talked about
questions that she'd had about

00:18:01.097 --> 00:18:02.867
how to do something in her job.

00:18:02.867 --> 00:18:03.887
And it was great.

00:18:03.887 --> 00:18:06.077
And it was a free flowing conversation

00:18:06.077 --> 00:18:09.627
in a way that I have not
experienced in a long time.

00:18:09.627 --> 00:18:12.517
And I think that that is something that,

00:18:12.517 --> 00:18:14.677
once we start getting that again,

00:18:14.677 --> 00:18:16.397
it's gonna be hard to give it up.

00:18:16.397 --> 00:18:19.517
So I'm not a believer in the full,

00:18:19.517 --> 00:18:20.637
in that everyone goes virtual.

00:18:20.637 --> 00:18:23.020
I just don't think it's sustainable.

00:18:24.527 --> 00:18:27.107
- Yeah, maybe some type
of hybrid, more flexible.

00:18:27.107 --> 00:18:31.307
I do want to double click
on the air travel thing

00:18:31.307 --> 00:18:34.077
because those folks who've
watched this in the past,

00:18:34.077 --> 00:18:35.957
know that myself and planes have not

00:18:35.957 --> 00:18:38.657
had a good history together. (chuckling)

00:18:38.657 --> 00:18:41.317
And, you know, even in my job,

00:18:41.317 --> 00:18:42.457
obviously I have to travel a lot,

00:18:42.457 --> 00:18:44.807
sometimes speak, sometimes,
you know, fundraise.

00:18:44.807 --> 00:18:47.167
And to your point, I
actually find that I'm able

00:18:47.167 --> 00:18:49.417
to have more efficient and
effective fundraising meetings

00:18:49.417 --> 00:18:51.597
with potential donors right now

00:18:51.597 --> 00:18:53.657
than before with all of that overhead.

00:18:53.657 --> 00:18:54.567
And it creates, you know,

00:18:54.567 --> 00:18:55.837
it's not good for the environment,

00:18:55.837 --> 00:18:59.237
it's not good for people's
health, et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:59.237 --> 00:19:02.756
Can we start a movement? (chuckling)

00:19:02.756 --> 00:19:04.747
- To never travel again?

00:19:04.747 --> 00:19:06.677
- Really, I mean, we should
travel to see our families,

00:19:06.677 --> 00:19:08.087
and, you know, that type of thing,

00:19:08.087 --> 00:19:09.457
go on vacation, see the world.

00:19:09.457 --> 00:19:11.287
But, you know, this business travel,

00:19:11.287 --> 00:19:13.807
let me go across country for a meeting

00:19:13.807 --> 00:19:16.197
and then, you know, schlep back.

00:19:16.197 --> 00:19:17.444
- Yeah, I'm with you.

00:19:17.444 --> 00:19:18.277
(audio distorting)

00:19:18.277 --> 00:19:19.807
- Let's start a LinkedIn movement.

00:19:19.807 --> 00:19:21.457
- I'm with you, I'm part
of that LinkedIn movement.

00:19:21.457 --> 00:19:22.430
There is no doubt.

00:19:23.397 --> 00:19:25.327
I think that, going back to
your last question though,

00:19:25.327 --> 00:19:30.327
I do think that it is easier
to have that kind of pledge

00:19:30.457 --> 00:19:32.447
right now when we are all acting,

00:19:32.447 --> 00:19:34.347
when we're all in this world.

00:19:34.347 --> 00:19:38.747
Once there is a big enough
shift towards in-person,

00:19:38.747 --> 00:19:40.087
everything goes in-person.

00:19:40.087 --> 00:19:42.017
So I do think there's some
kind of network effect

00:19:42.017 --> 00:19:42.967
that kind of happens

00:19:42.967 --> 00:19:45.177
when you just don't want to feel left out.

00:19:45.177 --> 00:19:47.430
Right now, you can't have FOMO today.

00:19:49.417 --> 00:19:51.517
There is no room where it happens anymore.

00:19:52.441 --> 00:19:55.617
It is a series of these small
rooms, virtually connected.

00:19:55.617 --> 00:19:57.647
Once that room where it
happens, the idea comes back,

00:19:57.647 --> 00:19:59.827
we're gonna all start getting back.

00:19:59.827 --> 00:20:01.937
The pull to get back on the plane

00:20:01.937 --> 00:20:03.487
is gonna start happening again.

00:20:03.487 --> 00:20:04.320
But I'm with you.

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:08.760
I got to say the one surprising
upside of this is, you know

00:20:10.117 --> 00:20:11.947
not to say that any of this is an upside,

00:20:11.947 --> 00:20:14.367
this is a horrible situation

00:20:14.367 --> 00:20:18.000
in the world we're living in
right now because of COVID,

00:20:19.187 --> 00:20:21.475
but I'm spending way
more time with my family.

00:20:21.475 --> 00:20:25.627
And it's really nice to not be
on a plane every other week.

00:20:25.627 --> 00:20:28.400
So I'm not looking forward
to going back to that.

00:20:29.727 --> 00:20:32.367
- No and, you know, we could
say that that's a nice to have,

00:20:32.367 --> 00:20:35.317
from just like your own personal
contentment point of view.

00:20:35.317 --> 00:20:37.127
But I believe having the
time with your family,

00:20:37.127 --> 00:20:38.217
having a better family life,

00:20:38.217 --> 00:20:40.317
connecting with your
kids, with your partner,

00:20:40.317 --> 00:20:42.567
that's gonna make you better,
more effective at work.

00:20:42.567 --> 00:20:44.767
And to your earlier
point, you can do more,

00:20:44.767 --> 00:20:46.997
in that same amount of time
it takes to go across country

00:20:46.997 --> 00:20:50.187
do one meeting and come back,
you could do 10 meetings,

00:20:50.187 --> 00:20:51.357
which will be far less big.

00:20:51.357 --> 00:20:52.857
Anyway, I'll get off.

00:20:52.857 --> 00:20:54.987
We'll go offline and
think about how we can-

00:20:54.987 --> 00:20:57.727
And you know, I have deep
sympathy for these industries

00:20:57.727 --> 00:20:59.807
and hopefully there's
ways for them to pivot.

00:20:59.807 --> 00:21:03.447
But you know, it has
never made sense to me,

00:21:03.447 --> 00:21:05.747
and it seems to make
everyone I'm talking to,

00:21:05.747 --> 00:21:07.267
they're like, yeah, my
life is so much better

00:21:07.267 --> 00:21:09.467
now that I don't have
to travel all the time.

00:21:09.467 --> 00:21:11.700
I was like, okay, let's
sign a pact right now.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.750
(laughing)
- Right, let's never go back.

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:16.547
- It's bad for the
environment, it really is.

00:21:16.547 --> 00:21:18.290
- I mean-
- Yeah, it is.

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:19.123
- It's horrible.

00:21:20.007 --> 00:21:23.097
So a lot of questions that are coming in,

00:21:23.097 --> 00:21:24.517
so one question I'll modify a little bit

00:21:24.517 --> 00:21:27.124
from Smart Bear on YouTube,

00:21:27.124 --> 00:21:29.917
"How do you think LinkedIn has
grown in the past five years?

00:21:29.917 --> 00:21:31.187
And what do you think
it's going to look like

00:21:31.187 --> 00:21:32.434
five years from now?"

00:21:34.697 --> 00:21:39.287
- The number one way I would
say it's grown is in terms

00:21:39.287 --> 00:21:44.287
of this explosion of
professional voice on LinkedIn.

00:21:47.937 --> 00:21:50.987
This idea of sharing what you know

00:21:50.987 --> 00:21:52.477
or sharing what you don't know,

00:21:52.477 --> 00:21:54.257
and saying and being open and vulnerable

00:21:54.257 --> 00:21:56.707
and saying I have
questions about how to do X

00:21:56.707 --> 00:21:58.987
or I don't understand why Y is happening.

00:21:58.987 --> 00:22:02.037
That was very different years ago.

00:22:02.037 --> 00:22:03.567
It was hard to convince people

00:22:03.567 --> 00:22:06.147
to write and share on LinkedIn

00:22:06.147 --> 00:22:08.977
under their professional
identity, which we talked about.

00:22:08.977 --> 00:22:10.257
It's no longer hard.

00:22:10.257 --> 00:22:11.967
I think people still
have to think about it,

00:22:11.967 --> 00:22:13.107
but it makes sense to them.

00:22:13.107 --> 00:22:14.707
And I think that a younger generation

00:22:14.707 --> 00:22:17.607
especially thinks of it as a given

00:22:19.773 --> 00:22:21.537
that their voice needs to be heard,

00:22:21.537 --> 00:22:23.640
that creating is part of who they are.

00:22:25.067 --> 00:22:26.197
And I think that's a great thing.

00:22:26.197 --> 00:22:28.847
This idea of democratizing
voice is something that

00:22:28.847 --> 00:22:31.507
I think is one of the
best things to happen.

00:22:31.507 --> 00:22:34.847
So before, when I was a
traditional journalist,

00:22:34.847 --> 00:22:38.837
and when I was doing
a story for "Fortune,"

00:22:38.837 --> 00:22:39.807
I would pick up the phone

00:22:39.807 --> 00:22:43.907
and I might make 100 phone
calls for an article.

00:22:43.907 --> 00:22:45.877
And then I would quote two people.

00:22:45.877 --> 00:22:48.987
And I would have benefited
from those 98 other phone calls

00:22:48.987 --> 00:22:50.927
and learning how an industry works

00:22:50.927 --> 00:22:52.717
or what's going on behind the scenes.

00:22:52.717 --> 00:22:55.397
But my readers never
got to see any of that.

00:22:55.397 --> 00:22:57.067
They would see it all
filtered through my eyes,

00:22:57.067 --> 00:22:59.347
but they never got the direct experience

00:22:59.347 --> 00:23:02.197
of those quotes from these experts.

00:23:02.197 --> 00:23:06.447
What we're seeing on LinkedIn
now is an outpouring of people

00:23:06.447 --> 00:23:09.527
who are experts or want
to develop an expertise,

00:23:09.527 --> 00:23:11.257
who are sharing what they know,

00:23:11.257 --> 00:23:13.590
which helps all of us get smarter faster.

00:23:14.677 --> 00:23:18.047
So that's probably been
the biggest change.

00:23:18.047 --> 00:23:19.407
And I think it mirrors,

00:23:19.407 --> 00:23:21.877
if you look at this thing
with Edelman, the PR firm,

00:23:21.877 --> 00:23:24.597
does something called the Edelman
Trust Barometer every year

00:23:24.597 --> 00:23:27.147
where they measure the entities

00:23:27.147 --> 00:23:29.147
and institutions that people trust today,

00:23:29.147 --> 00:23:31.590
it's a great read, you
should go check it out.

00:23:33.303 --> 00:23:38.114
The number one most trusted
entity today is my CEO.

00:23:39.277 --> 00:23:41.907
The people say my CEO, they're
the subject matter experts.

00:23:41.907 --> 00:23:43.917
And there has been this drop in trust

00:23:43.917 --> 00:23:47.547
in government and in the media.

00:23:47.547 --> 00:23:48.957
But people believe in the people

00:23:48.957 --> 00:23:50.567
who are signing their paychecks,

00:23:50.567 --> 00:23:53.587
and they believe in people who
have some skin in the game.

00:23:53.587 --> 00:23:55.237
And they want to hear more from them.

00:23:55.237 --> 00:23:57.467
And what we're seeing is
that these executives,

00:23:57.467 --> 00:23:58.397
these subject matter experts,

00:23:58.397 --> 00:23:59.477
these people who were just coming up

00:23:59.477 --> 00:24:01.357
are sharing this information on LinkedIn.

00:24:01.357 --> 00:24:02.190
So that's the biggest.

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:05.197
In terms of what I think we'll
see in the next five years,

00:24:05.197 --> 00:24:08.157
this kind of skills development
is, that we touched on,

00:24:08.157 --> 00:24:09.657
I think it's gonna be a massive part

00:24:09.657 --> 00:24:12.017
in what people use LinkedIn for.

00:24:12.017 --> 00:24:14.677
If you come to LinkedIn,
you come to develop skills,

00:24:14.677 --> 00:24:18.217
you use LinkedIn Learning
to get skilled up

00:24:18.217 --> 00:24:20.857
in how to do Facebook ads

00:24:20.857 --> 00:24:25.340
or how to learn R or
anything, Paddle or Python.

00:24:26.501 --> 00:24:27.767
And you put on your profile

00:24:27.767 --> 00:24:29.657
that you have developed that skill.

00:24:29.657 --> 00:24:34.037
And for recruiters,
being able to see that,

00:24:34.037 --> 00:24:38.747
you know, John Smith has a
skill in this area that we need,

00:24:38.747 --> 00:24:41.227
is an instant validator in a way

00:24:41.227 --> 00:24:42.977
that they haven't had before.

00:24:42.977 --> 00:24:44.227
And so that kind of skill development

00:24:44.227 --> 00:24:45.637
and putting skills on your profile,

00:24:45.637 --> 00:24:48.047
showing off what you know,
and how you've learned it,

00:24:48.047 --> 00:24:50.117
and then continuing to build off this,

00:24:50.117 --> 00:24:51.377
and how you think,

00:24:51.377 --> 00:24:53.227
which means like the postings

00:24:54.241 --> 00:24:55.707
and articles that people are publishing,

00:24:55.707 --> 00:25:00.707
that to me creates this explosion
of information on LinkedIn

00:25:01.067 --> 00:25:04.567
that enables people to change
jobs or find better jobs,

00:25:04.567 --> 00:25:06.887
or have the kind of
careers they want to have

00:25:08.687 --> 00:25:10.207
where they can guide it themselves.

00:25:10.207 --> 00:25:12.617
That's what I think is gonna happen.

00:25:12.617 --> 00:25:14.157
- Yeah, that makes a ton of sense.

00:25:14.157 --> 00:25:16.377
And related to that, this
question from Facebook,

00:25:16.377 --> 00:25:20.117
Maria Akeem asks, "What advice can I give

00:25:20.117 --> 00:25:21.807
my high schoolers graduating this year

00:25:21.807 --> 00:25:24.447
who are unsure about what
major or field to look into,

00:25:24.447 --> 00:25:26.924
given all the change that
we're talking about?"

00:25:27.997 --> 00:25:29.667
- Great question.

00:25:29.667 --> 00:25:34.207
All right, so I would say, number one,

00:25:34.207 --> 00:25:38.587
is this has been true for
years, the most in demand skill

00:25:38.587 --> 00:25:42.867
that we have heard from hiring managers

00:25:44.847 --> 00:25:47.927
is the ability to communicate.

00:25:47.927 --> 00:25:50.197
Communication skills
are the number one thing

00:25:50.197 --> 00:25:51.867
that hiring managers are looking for.

00:25:51.867 --> 00:25:53.807
And especially in a world like this,

00:25:53.807 --> 00:25:56.317
where we are all behind our computers,

00:25:56.317 --> 00:25:57.427
or writing on Google docs

00:25:57.427 --> 00:25:59.997
or however else we're communicating,

00:25:59.997 --> 00:26:04.107
this ability to be able to communicate

00:26:04.107 --> 00:26:06.990
clearly and consistently is important.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:10.847
So tell your high schooler, number one,

00:26:10.847 --> 00:26:13.917
make sure that you are
learning how to write,

00:26:13.917 --> 00:26:15.700
that you are very clear,

00:26:16.793 --> 00:26:19.248
that you're taking English lessons,

00:26:19.248 --> 00:26:21.217
that you are spending a lot of time

00:26:22.377 --> 00:26:24.177
learning how to communicate.

00:26:24.177 --> 00:26:25.667
Just be a great communicator.

00:26:25.667 --> 00:26:26.500
That's number one.

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:31.217
Number two, if you want to
look at the jobs of the future,

00:26:32.797 --> 00:26:33.630
we were just looking at,

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.587
sorry, I'm just pulling this up right now,

00:26:35.587 --> 00:26:37.637
we did a look at what are some of the jobs

00:26:37.637 --> 00:26:41.827
where we know that there
is outsized demand,

00:26:41.827 --> 00:26:44.087
and we expect that kind of
outsized demand to continue.

00:26:44.087 --> 00:26:47.027
So software developer,
sales representative,

00:26:47.027 --> 00:26:50.670
project manager, IT
administrator, digital marketer,

00:26:52.878 --> 00:26:54.317
IT support help desk,

00:26:54.317 --> 00:26:56.297
basically anything, if
you can be anything around

00:26:56.297 --> 00:26:58.600
how to make sure that people are-

00:26:59.447 --> 00:27:02.497
We know that technology is underpinning

00:27:02.497 --> 00:27:03.997
every single company today.

00:27:03.997 --> 00:27:05.517
Every company is a tech company.

00:27:05.517 --> 00:27:08.097
You want to have those
kinds of tech skills.

00:27:08.097 --> 00:27:12.487
So I think that an engineering
degree always makes sense.

00:27:12.487 --> 00:27:13.827
It might not be for everyone,

00:27:13.827 --> 00:27:18.827
but if that is an area that
you have any interest in,

00:27:18.937 --> 00:27:22.447
learning how to be an engineer
is a great thing to do.

00:27:22.447 --> 00:27:24.767
Otherwise, sales is always really great.

00:27:24.767 --> 00:27:25.817
So there's always gonna be a need

00:27:25.817 --> 00:27:28.077
for someone for doing sales.

00:27:28.077 --> 00:27:31.017
But if your teenager or high school grad

00:27:31.017 --> 00:27:32.917
doesn't know what he or she wants to do,

00:27:32.917 --> 00:27:34.827
just make sure that they are
learning how to communicate.

00:27:34.827 --> 00:27:35.660
That is the key.

00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:38.937
And the number two is make
sure that they are flexible,

00:27:38.937 --> 00:27:42.177
that they learn how to ride the waves.

00:27:42.177 --> 00:27:46.637
Because the kind of change
we see in industries today

00:27:46.637 --> 00:27:48.107
is not gonna slow down.

00:27:48.107 --> 00:27:51.587
And being able to adapt

00:27:53.207 --> 00:27:56.167
to situations around you
is gonna be so important.

00:27:56.167 --> 00:27:57.707
And being able to take
whatever you've learned,

00:27:57.707 --> 00:27:59.337
and then adapt it, and go somewhere else,

00:27:59.337 --> 00:28:00.947
you gotta know how to do that.

00:28:00.947 --> 00:28:03.497
That's probably more
important than any major

00:28:03.497 --> 00:28:05.117
that you can have today in college.

00:28:05.117 --> 00:28:06.637
It is just understanding

00:28:06.637 --> 00:28:09.007
how to adapt and how to stay flexible.

00:28:09.007 --> 00:28:10.607
That's what I would say.

00:28:10.607 --> 00:28:12.427
- If you were 22 years old,

00:28:12.427 --> 00:28:13.777
maybe you have an engineering
degree, maybe you have

00:28:13.777 --> 00:28:15.897
some other type of degree,
- I lost the sound.

00:28:15.897 --> 00:28:17.675
- Oh, can you hear me now?

00:28:17.675 --> 00:28:19.377
- I can't hear you.

00:28:19.377 --> 00:28:21.707
- Oh well, we're at the home stretch.

00:28:21.707 --> 00:28:22.707
- I think it was me.

00:28:23.647 --> 00:28:24.530
- It might be me.

00:28:25.396 --> 00:28:28.962
- Oh no.
- Let me see what the...

00:28:28.962 --> 00:28:30.795
Whoa, I think it's me.

00:28:32.889 --> 00:28:34.445
Can you hear me here?

00:28:34.445 --> 00:28:35.278
